
--> Lazy Loading Optimization
        What is Lazy Loading?
        Lazy loading means related entities are not fetched from the database until they are explicitly accessed.

        Example:
            @OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
            private List<Order> orders;

        Why Lazy Loading Exists
        ✅ Reduces initial query size
        ✅ Improves memory usage
        ✅ Avoids unnecessary joins

        Performance Issue with Lazy Loading
        Lazy loading becomes dangerous when:
        Accessed inside loops
        Accessed outside transactional context
        Used in REST APIs without planning
        This leads to:
        Multiple SQL queries
        Increased DB round trips
        Slower response time

        Common Symptoms
        Logs show many repeated SELECT statements
        Slow API response
        High DB CPU usage

--> N+1 Query Problem
        What is the N+1 Problem?
        Occurs when:
        1 query fetches parent entities
        N additional queries fetch child entities

        Example:
            List<Customer> customers = customerRepo.findAll();
            for(Customer c : customers) {
                c.getAddresses();
            }

        Result:
        1 query → customers
        N queries → addresses for each customer

        Why It Happens
        Default LAZY loading
        ORM unaware of future access
        Poor query planning

--> Database Indexing
        What is an Index?
        An index is a data structure (usually B-Tree) that speeds up data retrieval.
        Without index → Full table scan
        With index → Direct data access

        Why Indexing Improves Performance
        ✔ Faster SELECT queries
        ✔ Faster WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY
        ✔ Efficient search operations

        Types of Indexes
        🔹 Single Column Index
        🔹 Composite Index
        🔹 Unique Index

        Trade-offs of Indexing
        ⚠ Slows down INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE
        ⚠ Extra disk usage

--> Query Optimization
        Why Query Optimization Matters
        Poor queries cause:
        High latency
        High memory usage
        DB bottlenecks

        Common Query Mistakes
        ❌ SELECT *
        ❌ Fetching entire entities when only few fields needed
        ❌ No pagination
        ❌ Unnecessary joins

--> Connection Pooling – HikariCP
        What is Connection Pooling?
        Instead of creating a DB connection per request:
        Connections are reused
        Stored in a pool

        Why Connection Pooling is Critical
        Creating DB connections is:
        ❌ Expensive
        ❌ Slow
        ❌ Resource heavy

        Pooling gives:
        ✔ Faster response
        ✔ Controlled DB usage
        ✔ Better concurrency

        HikariCP Overview
        Default pool in Spring Boot
        Lightweight and very fast
        Low latency under high load

        Important HikariCP Properties
        | Property            | Purpose                  |
        | ------------------- | ------------------------ |
        | `maximumPoolSize`   | Max active connections   |
        | `minimumIdle`       | Minimum idle connections |
        | `connectionTimeout` | Max wait for connection  |
        | `idleTimeout`       | Close unused connections |



        Performance Issues Due to Poor Pooling
        ❌ Thread blocking
        ❌ DB connection exhaustion
        ❌ Request timeouts

        Advanced Performance Concepts
        SQL Logging
        Helps detect:
        N+1 queries
        Unnecessary joins
        Repeated selects

        Micrometer Metrics
        Used to monitor:
        DB connections
        Query latency
        JVM memory
        Thread usage



